== Match previews ==

=== Match 1: The Marauder vs. Katsuhiko Morita ===

The Marauder is 4-0 on Wednesday nights and has not been identified. He has defeated [[Katsuhiko Morita]], [[Shota Iwata]] (twice), and [[Yuto Ono]] in succession. Morita, a Seigigun veteran, requested the rematch after being the Marauder's first victim at [[NOPW Wednesday Episode 1|Episode 1]]. The pre-show match gives the Marauder a platform at a major event and may reveal more about his identity or intentions.

=== Match 2: Jin Tanaka (c) vs. Yuto Ono — World Television Championship ===

Tanaka won the World Television Championship at [[NOPW Wednesday Episode 5|Episode 5]] by defeating [[Tadayoshi Ishida]] with the help of the Cannon Club. At [[NOPW Wednesday Episode 6|Episode 6]], Ono defeated Tanaka in a non-title match — a result that shocked the Cannon Club and earned Ono a title shot. Tanaka called out Ono at [[NOPW Wednesday Episode 7|Episode 7]], declaring "You got lucky. At Fangatapu, I end you." Ono, a young Seigigun lion, has the momentum. Tanaka has the Cannon Club. In Fangatapu, the crowd will be overwhelmingly behind Ono and hostile to the Cannon Club champion.

=== Match 3: Kai Nakamura & Danny Viggo (c) vs. Tetsuya Yamamoto & Akira Sato — Junior Tag Team Championship ===

Nakamura and Viggo, the Cannon Club junior tag champions, have been losing to Ono and Yamamoto in consecutive non-title tag matches. With Ono challenging for the World Television Championship, Yamamoto teams with CHAOS junior Akira Sato — who pushed Tanaka to the limit at Episode 7 — to challenge for the junior tag titles. The makeshift team has no established chemistry, but both men are ascending and the Cannon Club's junior division is under siege.

=== Match 4: CHAOS (c) vs. Big Stan, Mamadou Diallo & Malo — Openweight 6-Man Tag Team Championship ===

CHAOS — Kawada Masahiro, Takagi Yuto, and Kazuki Fujinami — have been dominant as 6-man tag champions. Big Stan, Mamadou Diallo, and Malo Vaiahi have lost three consecutive tag matches to CHAOS on Wednesday nights. The Cannon Club power team is desperate. With Kengo focused on the Strong Openweight Championship and the Usos defending the tag titles, this is the Cannon Club's last chance to prove its depth at Island Invasion.

=== Match 5: Ryohei Maeda vs. Daigo Kuroki — "Kuroshio Remnant" ===

The two surviving members of Kuroshio — other than Leoni and KAZUKI — face each other in a match that is as much about grief as it is about competition. Maeda was defeated by KAZUKI at Episode 6. Kuroki was defeated by KAZUKI at Episode 7. Both men have been left behind by the dissolution of the bond. The match is being billed as the "Kuroshio Remnant" — a contest between two men who lost the same thing and are trying to figure out what they are without it. The outcome may determine whether the remnants of Kuroshio find common ground or go their separate ways.

=== Match 6: The Usos (c) vs. Diego Reyes & Haruto Kimura — Tag Team Championship, No Disqualification ===

The Usos have retained the IGO Tag Team Championship twice by non-clean finishes — a DQ at Episode 5 and a no contest at Episode 7. Reyes and Kimura have attacked the Usos before the bell, brawled through arenas, and made it clear they will use any means necessary to take the titles. The no-disqualification stipulation removes the Usos' ability to escape by breaking the rules. The challengers — CHAOS's Diego "The Cobra" Reyes and Haruto "The Wind" Kimura — have the momentum, the violence, and the crowd behind them. The Usos have the experience and the Cannon Club. This is the most violent match on the card.

=== Match 7: Taumafa Vaiahi (c) vs. Kengo — Strong Openweight Championship ===

The Vaiahi family war reaches the ring. Taumafa, the Strong Openweight Champion and the Giant of the family, has defeated Malo twice and challenged Kengo directly. Kengo, the Cannon Club leader, has sent proxies — Malo, Big Stan, Diallo — but has not answered the challenge in the ring. At Fangatapu, he can no longer avoid it. The match is deeply personal: Kengo turned on the Vaiahi family when he founded the Cannon Club in 2019. Taumafa stayed loyal to the family. Now, in the family's homeland, the hierarchy will be decided. Kengo has the Cannon Club behind him. Taumafa has the crowd. The Strong Openweight Championship is the prize, but the family name is the stakes.

=== Match 8: Marcus Vance (c) vs. Jusuf Muntasir — Intercontinental Championship ===

Marcus Vance enters this match as a lone babyface — betrayed by the Cannon Club, attacked by Jusuf, and carrying the Intercontinental Championship alone. Jusuf Muntasir, the Baliscan oil wrestler who declared professional wrestling "a lie," has dominated Vance with his unfamiliar mosaraza grappling style and used a low blow to cause a disqualification at Episode 7. The match is deeply personal: Vance is defending not just the title but the sport itself against a man who views it with contempt. The Cannon Club's involvement is the wildcard — Kengo has declared the title "belongs to us," and the faction may interfere to cost Vance the championship or to ensure Jusuf takes it. Vance has no allies. The crowd in Fangatapu — which has never cheered for a Cannon Club member — may be the only support he has.

=== Match 9: Leoni Masua (c) vs. KAZUKI — Heavyweight Championship ===

The main event. The defining feud of the era. Leoni Masua, the Tribal Chief, the two-time IGO Heavyweight Champion, defending the namo in his homeland against KAZUKI — the Shooting Star, the man who ended the 1,095-day reign, the man who has now defeated both of Leoni's former Kuroshio brothers. This is their fourth major singles encounter. KAZUKI won the first two — January 3, 2025, and the New Beginning in July 2025. Leoni won the third — the Shendan Cup on June 12, 2026, catching the Twin Star Press and delivering the Spear of Fangatapu. Now, in Fangatapu, with the Vaiahi crowd behind him, Leoni defends the title for the first time of his second reign. KAZUKI has no allies. Leoni has no former brothers. The conundrum — whether to help Vance, whether to fight Jusuf, whether the shifting battlefield affects his focus — may be the champion's greatest vulnerability. But the namo is home. The Spear of Fangatapu was born on this island. And the crowd will be the loudest NOPW has ever heard.

== Cultural significance ==

The 2026 Island Invasion Tour is the first to be held since the Fono of Vaiahi declared a national holiday following Leoni's Shendan Cup victory. The event is expected to draw visitors from across the Vaiahi diaspora, the Shendan atolls, and Okaiken. The Fangatapu National Stadium, the largest venue in the Vaiahi Islands, is being prepared with traditional Vaiahi ceremony elements — the kava ceremony, the taualuga war dance, and the wayfinding star compass (kāinga whetū) — integrated into the production. Leoni's entrance is expected to be the most elaborate of his career, with the full ceremony restored.

The event also marks the first time the Cannon Club — the faction that Kengo Vaiahi built by turning on the Vaiahi family — will perform in Fangatapu. The crowd's reaction to Kengo, the prodigal son who became the enemy, is one of the most anticipated moments of the night.

